# Ask clear questions on pages and in dialogs

**Reference pattern.** These examples are local design guidance. They do not install new Home Assistant screens or perform device actions.

A question should earn its place in a household workflow. Explain why the answer is needed and avoid asking again for information the person already supplied.

## The NHS principle we adapt

The [NHS question-page pattern](https://service-manual.nhs.uk/design-system/patterns/question-pages) starts with one question, a clear heading, an input, Back and Continue. It also explains when related questions belong together and why a slider needs an alternative to dragging. civica uses this for setup pages and adapts the same clarity to short, accessible dialogs.

## Ask one understandable thing

For a single question, use the heading as the input label or group legend. Explain the consequence in a short hint. Offer “No room yet” or “I do not know” where that is a valid answer, rather than forcing a fabricated selection. Retain answers when people go back.

<%= example('nhs-question-room') %>

## Use a dialog for a short contextual edit

A small change such as a display name can stay near its triggering control. Use a real accessible dialog with a visible heading and an explicit Cancel action. Choose a full page for a long form, complex help, multiple dependent steps or content that needs a useful bookmark.

<%= example('nhs-question-modal') %>

On opening, place focus at a sensible element inside the dialog. Keep keyboard focus contained while it is modal, allow Escape to cancel, and return focus to the original trigger on close. A long dialog must scroll within the viewport at 320 pixels and high zoom; its close or cancel control must remain reachable. Do not rely on clicking the backdrop to escape.

## Correct errors without losing work

Show an error summary and an associated field message after a failed submit. Retain the attempted answer. Distinguish a malformed value from unavailable data or a permission failure. Do not show field errors before the person has had a chance to answer.

<%= example('nhs-question-error') %>

## Accessibility and content

Keep visible labels, hints and errors programmatically associated. Use actual radios, checkboxes and inputs rather than clickable text that imitates them. Avoid an unnecessary progress bar; only show a total step count when conditional branches make that total reliable. For a continuous value, retain native keyboard operation and provide an equivalent input or non-drag control where needed.

## Home Assistant implementation boundary

Preserve supported native entity controls rather than replacing their behaviour with a styled fake. A display-name edit must not rename an entity unless the interface explicitly promises that operation. A brightness or temperature question must use the actual device capabilities, units and permitted range. Recheck permission and availability before submission, then announce the real result.

The examples submit only to local preview feedback. They do not save a profile, rename an entity or send a service call. In a production dialog, closing after a save should wait for the appropriate confirmed result; closing an unsaved edit must not apply it silently.
